# Copy to .env. Never commit the real file; .gitignore already excludes it.
#
# Variables marked LATER are read by no code yet; the tag names the milestone
# that starts reading each one. Everything else is live. Setting a LATER variable
# does nothing today, but it is not silent about it: startup logs one warning
# listing every LATER variable you have set, along with its milestone, because a
# knob that is documented and ignored is indistinguishable from a knob that is
# broken.
#
# Every live variable is validated at startup and a bad value is a startup error,
# not a fallback to the default. That includes booleans: PEREGRINE_ENABLE_X=ture
# fails loudly rather than reading as "off", because "the value was not
# understood so the feature is disabled" is exactly how autonomous posting stayed
# dark for months. Errors are reported all at once, so a six-variable mistake
# takes one restart to see rather than six.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- credentials

# Discord Developer Portal -> Bot -> Token.
#
# Peregrine needs the MESSAGE CONTENT privileged intent, which must be ticked
# under Bot in the Developer Portal. Without it Discord refuses the connection
# and the bot cannot read the messages it exists to learn from.
DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN=

# A Discord user ID that gates operator-only commands. Fails CLOSED: while this
# is empty those commands are refused for everyone, never allowed for everyone.
# This replaces a user ID that was hardcoded in main.go as the only
# authorization check in the codebase.
PEREGRINE_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_USER_ID=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- storage

# Where the bbolt corpus lives.
#
# Must be an absolute path inside the mounted volume when running in a
# container. The image runs with a read-only root filesystem, so the bare
# relative default resolves against the distroless working directory and
# bbolt.Open fails. This is the single most common way to get a bot that starts,
# appears healthy, and has silently learned nothing.
PEREGRINE_DB_PATH=/data/markov.db

# In-process corpus snapshots, taken inside a bbolt read transaction via tx.WriteTo
# so they are consistent by construction and do not block writers. There is no
# backup sidecar on purpose: see the comment in docker-compose.prod.yml for why
# `cp markov.db` is not a backup at all.
#
# EMPTY DISABLES THEM, and empty is the default. There is no safe guess for a path,
# and writing megabytes somewhere the operator did not choose is worse than not
# backing up.
#
# In a container this must be on a WRITABLE MOUNT. The image runs read_only, so
# any path outside a volume or bind fails on the first write, and a relative path
# resolves against the distroless working directory. docker-compose.prod.yml binds
# ./backups to /backups for this, deliberately outside the corpus volume so that
# losing or removing that volume does not take the snapshots with it: set
# PEREGRINE_BACKUP_DIR=/backups.
PEREGRINE_BACKUP_DIR=

# Each snapshot is a full copy of the corpus, so the disk cost is KEEP times the
# corpus size, on the same volume as the corpus.
#
# Retention only ever removes files this service named, and never removes anything
# after a snapshot that failed: pruning on a schedule while backups quietly fail
# deletes every good copy one tick at a time.
PEREGRINE_BACKUP_TICK=24h
PEREGRINE_BACKUP_KEEP=7

# ------------------------------------------------------------- tuning export

# The tuning export writes one JSON object per line describing what the bot said,
# why it said it, and whether anybody reacted. It exists because SPEC.md section 10
# has five open decisions all waiting on the same sentence, "revisit against real
# ingested text": the only instrument that can judge output today is the golden
# harness, and that runs against a 150-line synthetic fixture.
#
# The loop is: the bot writes here, the operator pulls the directory down, and
# `go run ./cmd/bot -tuning-report ./tuning` summarizes it. That mode reads no
# corpus, needs no token and contacts nothing, so it runs anywhere.
#
# EMPTY DISABLES IT, and empty is the default, for the same reason as the backups
# above: no safe guess for a path, and the same read_only container caveat applies.
# docker-compose.prod.yml binds ./tuning to /tuning, so set
# PEREGRINE_TUNING_DIR=/tuning there. THE BIND NEEDS THE UID TREATMENT the backups
# need, or every write fails a tick after startup where nobody watching the boot
# logs will see it:
#
#   sudo chgrp 65532 tuning && chmod 775 tuning
#
# WHAT LANDS IN HERE IS MESSAGE TEXT: the prompts people typed and the replies the
# bot sent. Treat the directory like the corpus rather than like logs. What does
# NOT land in here is anything the emit gate refused, which is deliberate and
# matches internal/safety never recording the offending content anywhere.
PEREGRINE_TUNING_DIR=

# File lifecycle. Names are fixed-width UTC, so they sort chronologically, and
# retention only ever removes files this service named and never after a failed
# write. Same three rules as the backups, and each is a way to lose the archive.
PEREGRINE_TUNING_ROTATE=24h
PEREGRINE_TUNING_KEEP=14

# The share of generation attempts recorded. 1.0 records everything, which is right
# for a bot that only speaks when addressed. Lowering it makes the file smaller and
# makes rare outcomes unanswerable, so lower it only if the archive is genuinely too
# large to move.
PEREGRINE_TUNING_SAMPLE=1.0

# How long a sent reply is watched for reactions and answers, and how many replies
# may be watched at once. The second is a bound on a map keyed by message ID, which
# is a leak this repository has shipped twice; going over it resolves the OLDEST
# watch early rather than dropping the newest, so no record is lost, it just gets a
# shorter window (which the record itself reports).
PEREGRINE_TUNING_ENGAGEMENT_WINDOW=10m
PEREGRINE_TUNING_TRACK_MAX=500

# Stamped on every exported record so two archives can be told apart, and so the
# report can refuse to average across a change it cannot see.
#
# An environment variable rather than a build stamp because .dockerignore excludes
# .git, so debug.ReadBuildInfo has no vcs revision inside the image. In production
# docker-compose.prod.yml passes the image tag, which CI sets to the commit SHA it
# built, so this is only worth setting by hand for a local run.
PEREGRINE_VERSION=dev

# -------------------------------------------------------------------- safety

# The blocklist is data, not source: committing an explicit slur and
# illegal-content pattern list would make this repo a searchable copy of one,
# and would turn every addition into a rebuild and a public diff instead of an
# edit the operator can make mid-incident. See blocklist.example.txt.
#
# Fails CLOSED once set. A missing file, an unreadable file, a malformed line, an
# uncompilable pattern and an EMPTY file are all startup errors, because a partial
# ruleset is indistinguishable from a working one right up until the worst possible
# moment. Every bad line is reported in one pass, with its line number.
#
# Leaving it UNSET is allowed and warns loudly. The built-in baseline in
# internal/filter still applies, so a developer on a scratch corpus does not have to
# invent a slur list first, but the operator list is where the threat and
# illegal-content patterns live and it is the only part editable without a deploy.
# Do not run in a hostile channel without it.
#
# Patterns are matched against the NORMALIZED form of the text, never the raw form,
# so you do not need to enumerate leet spellings, spacing tricks or homoglyphs.
PEREGRINE_BLOCKLIST_PATH=/blocklist.txt

# Emergency stop. When set to 1/true/yes/on, every outbound message is refused
# process-wide while the bot keeps reading and learning. This is the host-level
# lever for when the bot is actively saying something awful in a live channel and
# waiting for a deploy is not acceptable. Takes effect on restart, and says so in
# the log at startup so a silent bot is never a mystery.
#
# It deliberately does NOT stop learning. During an incident the output is the
# problem, not the corpus, and stopping ingestion would also stop the bot noticing
# what is being said to it.
PEREGRINE_PAUSE_ALL_WRITES=

# How many DISTINCT authors must have produced a continuation before the bot is
# willing to generate it. This is the main anti-poisoning control: raw frequency
# means repeating a phrase is a direct write to the model, so without this one
# determined user can teach the bot to say anything. Requiring independent
# authors makes that attack need collusion instead of persistence.
#
# Too high and a quiet server generates nothing; too low and two accounts defeat
# it. Start at 2 and tune against the real active-user count.
#
# LIVE as of M7a. 0 disables the gate, which is the right value on a scratch
# corpus and a deliberate choice on a live one. Know the consequence on a YOUNG
# corpus: until several people have said similar things most continuations have a
# single author, so the bot is close to silent. That is the control working, not
# a fault, and the fix is a corpus with more people in it rather than a lower
# threshold.
PEREGRINE_MIN_DISTINCT_AUTHORS=2

# ------------------------------------------------------- generation, quality

# Sampling temperature. Higher is more chaotic. This replaces a knob called
# "Creativity" whose arithmetic inverted its own name: it was applied as an
# exponent of 1/(Creativity+0.01), so raising it toward 1 could only ever
# approach an exponent of 1.0 and never pass it, meaning the dial could not
# reach the interesting half of its own range and the sampler collapsed toward
# argmax. A chaos bot that is accidentally deterministic is the worst outcome
# here, which is why this got rewritten rather than renamed.
#
# There is deliberately no PEREGRINE_CREATIVITY, then or now. Creativity stayed a
# constant in the code rather than becoming a variable, because exposing a knob
# whose arithmetic contradicts its name would invite an operator to tune something
# broken and conclude the bot is unresponsive to it.
#
# LIVE as of M7a, range 0 to 10. 0 is argmax, which is the degenerate end of the
# dial and is roughly what the old engine did by accident. 1.0 is the model as
# estimated. Past about 3 the output is word salad even with top-k on, and the
# upper bound is generous precisely so you can see that for yourself.
PEREGRINE_TEMPERATURE=1.0

# Bonus applied to a candidate that appeared in the prompt.
#
# ITS UNITS CHANGED IN M7a AND THE OLD DEFAULT IS NOW REFUSED AT STARTUP. It used
# to be added to an unnormalized score whose scale was raw n-gram counts, so 15.0
# was sensible. Scoring is a log-probability now, where an additive 15.0
# multiplies a candidate's odds by three million and makes echoing the prompt the
# only thing the bot ever does. The valid range is 0 to 5.
#
# The variable deliberately kept its name so that a stale 15.0 in your .env is a
# startup error naming the new range. Renaming it would have let the old value
# quietly stop being read, which is indistinguishable from the bot ignoring its
# configuration.
PEREGRINE_PROMPT_RELEVANCE_BOOST=0.6

# Truncate the candidate tail before sampling. This is what keeps a high
# temperature surprising rather than word salad: cut the tail, then sample the
# surviving head hot. 0 disables.
#
# LIVE as of M7a. Applied first, then TOP_P over what survives.
PEREGRINE_TOP_K=40
# Nucleus threshold: keep the shortest leading run of candidates whose combined
# probability reaches this. 1 disables. LIVE as of M7a.
PEREGRINE_TOP_P=0.95

# Kneser-Ney absolute discount, subtracted from each observed count.
#
# LIVE as of M7a, and it must stay below 1: at 1 or above it erases every
# count-1 continuation, and in a corpus this sparse that is nearly all of them.
PEREGRINE_KN_DISCOUNT=0.75

# How far to pull Kneser-Ney back toward raw counts, 0.0 to 1.0.
#
# This knob exists because textbook KN actively fights this bot's purpose. KN
# estimates lower orders from CONTINUATION counts, the number of distinct
# contexts a token follows, which correctly demotes a word like "Francisco" that
# is common but nearly always preceded by "San". The problem is that a meme, a
# copypasta and an inside joke are statistically indistinguishable from
# "Francisco": high frequency, few distinct contexts. So pure KN would
# systematically suppress exactly the register this server runs on.
#
# 0.0 is textbook KN and maximizes conventional quality. 1.0 is raw counts.
# 0.25 leans to KN while keeping the memes. Tune on golden samples, not vibes.
#
# LIVE as of M7a. Setting this to 0 on the authority of a paper makes the output
# worse by the only metric that matters here.
PEREGRINE_KN_RAW_MIX=0.25

# Generated sentence length, in words. The old bound was 30 + rand(15), which
# is a paragraph: too long to land in a chat channel. Short and punchy reads as
# a joke, long reads as a malfunction.
#
# LIVE as of M7b, and these are now the ONLY length mechanism: a target is
# sampled between them once per sentence, skewed toward the floor, and the end
# token is penalized below it and rewarded past it. There used to be three
# mechanisms that could disagree.
#
# Below two words the bot stays silent rather than posting, because a one-word
# reply reads as a malfunction rather than as a short joke.
PEREGRINE_MIN_WORDS=4
PEREGRINE_MAX_WORDS=18

# Highest n-gram order. Minimum 2: order 1 produced an empty prefix, which put
# the entire vocabulary into a single database key that nothing ever read.
PEREGRINE_MAX_NGRAM=5

# Window for word co-occurrence, in words. Replaces an all-pairs O(n^2) loop
# that ran inside the write transaction. 0 means unbounded.
#
# LIVE as of M7b. This is a model change as much as an optimization, and in the
# right direction: "co-occurs anywhere in the same message" gets weaker as
# messages get longer, because a 200-word message links every word in it to every
# other. Proximity is what association actually means. It is also a real cost fix,
# since the loop runs inside the single write transaction that serializes all
# ingestion: at 200 words, all-pairs is nearly 40,000 read-add-writes and a window
# of 5 is about 2,000.
PEREGRINE_COOCCURRENCE_WINDOW=5

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- persona

# Chance that a direct mention gets the roast persona rather than a neutral
# reply, 0.0 to 1.0.
#
# LIVE as of M7b, where this was a hardcoded 0.10. The persona is one mechanism
# now: it biases candidate selection toward roast vocabulary AND decides the
# post-pass filler, rather than two independent coin flips that could disagree.
# A self-mention without a direct address is always a roast regardless of this.
PEREGRINE_ROAST_CHANCE=0.10

# What makes the bot answer unprompted when it hears its own name. Must compile
# as a Go regexp.
PEREGRINE_SELF_MENTION_PATTERN=(?i)\b(peregrine|bird)\b

# -------------------------------------------------------- engagement: aggro

# Hourly roll to pick a random active user and react to their messages for a
# while. Pure chaos, and the cheapest kind in the bot.
PEREGRINE_AGGRO_TICK=1h
PEREGRINE_AGGRO_CHANCE=0.20
PEREGRINE_AGGRO_DURATION=20m
PEREGRINE_AGGRO_EMOJI=🐦

# How far back a user counts as "around" when a target is picked.
#
# It reads the in-process activity tracker as of M11b, so the practical bound is
# also how long the process has been up: for the first minutes after a restart
# there is no target at all. That is the right answer for a feature whose point
# is poking somebody who is present. The version this replaced paged six hours of
# Discord history per tick and could pick someone who had long since left.
PEREGRINE_AGGRO_ACTIVITY_WINDOW=6h

# How recent traffic must be for a channel to count as somewhere worth speaking
# unprompted. Read by autonomous posting and by interval-mode word games, the two
# features that choose a channel for themselves.
#
# This role used to belong to PEREGRINE_INGEST_LOOKBACK, which was a misuse: the
# lookback bounds the history walk and has nothing to say about where people are
# talking now.
PEREGRINE_ACTIVE_CHANNEL_WINDOW=1h

# -------------------------------------------------------- engagement: images

# The off switch gates URL capture as well as reposting, not just the repost.
# With reposting off, the cache has no consumer, so continuing to store other
# people's media URLs in the operator's database would be liability with no
# upside.
PEREGRINE_ENABLE_IMAGE_REPOST=true

# Two rates, because there are two situations. The ambient one is deliberately
# the higher: when the bot is already answering a mention it is contributing to
# the channel anyway, so an unrelated image on top of that reply is noise rather
# than chaos.
PEREGRINE_IMAGE_REPOST_CHANCE=0.015
PEREGRINE_IMAGE_REPOST_CHANCE_DIRECT=0.01
PEREGRINE_IMAGE_CACHE_SIZE=100

# Cap per author so one user cannot fill the cache and turn the bot into their
# personal reposting service. The bot republishes these under its own name, so
# the operator wears whatever it posts (SPEC.md section 4, A7).
#
# It MUST be below PEREGRINE_IMAGE_CACHE_SIZE, and a value at or above it is a
# startup error naming both: a cap that permits one person to hold every entry
# is not a cap, and an operator who believes the protection is on while it does
# nothing is worse off than one who knows it is off.
#
# The cache is keyed by source message as of M11b, which is also what lets a
# deletion revoke a cached URL: deleting a message is a strong signal that its
# content should not be republished, and the bot reposting something a moderator
# just removed is the worst version of this feature.
PEREGRINE_IMAGE_MAX_PER_AUTHOR=5

# ------------------------------------------------- engagement: autonomous post

# Posting unprompted. Comma-separated channel IDs, and an empty list means no
# channels: a bot that speaks unprompted must never do so somewhere nobody
# named. Enabling the feature with an empty list is a STARTUP ERROR rather than
# silence, because the previous arrangement had the feature disabled AND the
# list empty, so flipping either one alone produced nothing and no explanation.
PEREGRINE_ENABLE_AUTONOMOUS_POST=false
PEREGRINE_AUTONOMOUS_POST_CHANNELS=

# Its own ticker. Both this loop and the history backfill were driven by one
# AutonomyTick constant, so tuning how often the bot re-read history also changed
# how often it spoke unprompted: two unrelated decisions on one dial, and the
# second one invisible. Defaults to the 10m they used to share.
PEREGRINE_AUTONOMOUS_POST_TICK=10m

# Chance to skip a tick entirely, for pacing that does not look mechanical.
PEREGRINE_AUTONOMOUS_SKIP_CHANCE=0.90

# ---------------------------------------------------- engagement: word games

# ON by default as of M11. Word games were switched off by a compile-time
# constant, then by a flag defaulting false, and a game nobody can play is not a
# conservative default: it is a feature that does not exist. The failure mode of
# having it on is that the bot occasionally posts a puzzle, which is the point.
PEREGRINE_ENABLE_WORD_GAMES=true

# How games start.
#
#   activity  when a channel has been busy: THRESHOLD messages within WINDOW,
#             then a TRIGGER_CHANCE roll per message. The bot joins in.
#   interval  on a timer, in the busiest channel it can see. The bot interrupts.
#
# activity is the default as of M11, because a puzzle arriving while people are
# talking reads as participation and one arriving on a schedule reads as noise.
PEREGRINE_WORDGAME_FREQUENCY_MODE=activity

# Interval mode only. 30m, not the 2m that stood here, which was plainly a
# leftover from testing: a puzzle every two minutes is the bot talking over the
# conversation. The minimum is 5m for the same reason.
#
# In M11 this began pacing WORD GAMES. Until then it paced the autonomous poster,
# which posts Markov sentences, so this dial controlled how often the bot said
# something that was not a word game at all (SPEC.md finding 30).
PEREGRINE_WORDGAME_INTERVAL=30m

# How long players get to answer.
PEREGRINE_WORDGAME_TIMEOUT=1m

# How long a win or timeout announcement survives before the bot tidies it away.
# 0 keeps them, which is what an operator who wants a visible history should set.
PEREGRINE_WORDGAME_ANNOUNCE_TTL=30s

# How far into a puzzle the first letter is revealed. 0 turns hints off.
#
# The hint EDITS the original announcement rather than posting a second message,
# so it arrives where people are already looking, and it costs no timer: the
# sweep that already expires games collects due hints as well.
#
# It must be below TIMEOUT, and a value at or above it is a startup error naming
# both variables rather than a clamp. A hint due after the puzzle has ended never
# fires, which is a knob wired to nothing: the operator sets it, nothing happens,
# and the feature gets blamed for ignoring configuration.
PEREGRINE_WORDGAME_HINT_AFTER=30s

# Activity mode only: a game starts once a channel has seen THRESHOLD messages
# within WINDOW, then on a TRIGGER_CHANCE roll per message. All three were
# literals buried in the message handler before M11.
#
# The counting itself is the shared activity tracker's as of M11b, but the window
# stays here, because how busy is busy enough for a WORD GAME is this feature's
# judgement. Aggro asks the same tracker a different question over a different
# window, which is why it keeps timestamps rather than one counter.
#
# THRESHOLD is capped at 100, which is a ceiling rather than an opinion: the
# tracker keeps a fixed ring of message times per channel, so a count saturates
# and a higher threshold could never be met. The variable would be accepted, the
# trigger would never fire, and nothing would say why.
PEREGRINE_WORDGAME_ACTIVITY_WINDOW=5m
PEREGRINE_WORDGAME_ACTIVITY_THRESHOLD=5
PEREGRINE_WORDGAME_TRIGGER_CHANCE=0.025

# Puzzle word length, in RUNES. The old filter compared BYTES, so an accented
# five-letter word counted as six.
#
# Words are also rejected for containing anything other than letters, and for
# having only one distinct letter: "aaaaa" cannot be scrambled into anything
# different from itself, and the old scrambler's response to that was to recurse
# until the stack died. That was unreachable with the embedded list and reachable
# with a custom one, so it could only ever have fired in production.
PEREGRINE_WORDGAME_MIN_LENGTH=5
PEREGRINE_WORDGAME_MAX_LENGTH=12

# How often expired puzzles and due deletions are swept.
#
# This is the resolution of the timeout, so a game can outlive its deadline by up
# to one tick. That trade is deliberate: a puzzle ending a few seconds late is
# invisible, whereas the goroutine-per-game this replaces outlived shutdown and
# then logged failures against a closed session.
PEREGRINE_WORDGAME_SWEEP_TICK=5s

# Overrides the embedded word list. Empty uses the embedded one, which
# is the normal case and cannot fail at runtime.
PEREGRINE_WORDGAME_DICTIONARY=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- ingest

# Backfill from channel history. The old loop re-scanned the whole trailing 24h
# every 10 minutes and deduplicated against a 10,000-key window, so on a busy
# server older messages were evicted and then learned AGAIN, double-counting
# n-grams. M9 replaced the rescan with per-channel high-water cursors.
PEREGRINE_INGEST_TICK=10m

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HISTORY REPAIR. Default off. You run this once after a writer bug is fixed,
# and then turn it off again.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Some indexes cannot be rebuilt from the corpus. The corpus stores n-grams and
# counts and never message text, so anything derived from message STRUCTURE, such
# as the two co-occurrence indexes, needs the original word sequences with their
# positions. Fixing the writer does not fix the data those indexes already have,
# and the ingest cursor is monotonic so the normal pass never re-reads history.
#
# A repair walks history once and writes ONLY its own index: no n-grams, no dedup
# entries, no stats, no name records, because re-reading through the normal path
# would count every n-gram a second time.
#
# Known jobs, and "all" to run every one:
#
#   associations   the backfill wrote no co-occurrence associations before M14
#
# An unknown name is a startup error listing the ones that exist.
PEREGRINE_REPAIR_JOBS=

# The instant a fix deployed, RFC3339. NORMALLY LEAVE THIS EMPTY.
#
# The corpus records when each generation of the write path first ran, and a
# repair reads that stamp to know which messages predate the fix it repairs. That
# is what makes a repair additive rather than destructive: only older messages are
# walked, so nothing is counted twice.
#
# This override exists for exactly ONE case and should not grow a second. The
# association fix shipped in M14, before generation stamping existed, so its real
# start instant is unrecoverable and only you know it. If you need to run the
# associations job on a corpus that predates M18, set this to the deploy time of
# the M14 release; a repair with no stamp and no override declines and says so.
#
# Erring EARLY is the cheaper mistake: a small double count is bounded by tanh in
# every consumer, whereas stopping short leaves the gap the repair exists to close.
PEREGRINE_REPAIR_BEFORE=


# LOOKBACK IS A BOOTSTRAP BOUND AS OF M9, NOT A RE-READ WINDOW.
#
# It applies to the FIRST pass over a channel, to stop that pass walking the
# channel's entire history, and never again: later passes resume from a stored
# cursor. Raising it makes the very first ingest reach further back and costs
# nothing thereafter. Lowering it does nothing at all for a channel the bot has
# already read, which is the one surprising consequence worth knowing.
PEREGRINE_INGEST_LOOKBACK=24h

# How many guilds and how many channels within a guild to walk at once.
#
# LIVE as of M9. Both were unbounded: one goroutine per channel per guild, each
# paging Discord, which earns rate limits whose retries then make it worse. Keep
# them low. bbolt serializes every write process-wide, so past a handful of
# workers the extra concurrency buys contention rather than throughput.
PEREGRINE_INGEST_GUILD_CONCURRENCY=4
PEREGRINE_INGEST_CHANNEL_CONCURRENCY=4
PEREGRINE_INGEST_BATCH_DELAY=500ms

# Channels the bot never POSTS in, comma-separated IDs.
#
# LIVE as of M10a, and enforced inside the send chokepoint rather than in the reply
# logic: an operator setting this means "not in there", not "not in reply to a
# message in there", so the autonomous poster and the word games respect it too.
#
# It deliberately does NOT stop learning from those channels. That is the same
# asymmetry PEREGRINE_PAUSE_ALL_WRITES has and for the same reason: what causes
# trouble is the output. If you want a channel excluded from the corpus as well,
# that is a different control and it does not exist yet.
PEREGRINE_IGNORE_CHANNELS=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ runtime

# Bounded worker pool for incoming messages. Replaced an unbounded goroutine per
# message that shared a WaitGroup with the shutdown path, which could panic on
# exit and let handlers keep using the database after it closed. A full queue
# drops and counts rather than blocking, because best-effort chat is the honest
# semantics and an unbounded queue is just a slower crash. The drop total is
# reported on shutdown and in the status line.
#
# Keep the worker count low. bbolt serializes every write transaction
# process-wide, so past a handful of workers the extra concurrency buys lock
# contention rather than throughput.
PEREGRINE_MESSAGE_WORKERS=4
PEREGRINE_MESSAGE_QUEUE=256

PEREGRINE_STATUS_TICK=5m

# The Discord presence line: "watching 412,033 phrases it has picked up", rotated
# on the status tick above. It has no interval of its own on purpose, because the
# numbers come from the corpus page walk that tick already pays for.
#
# ON by default, which almost nothing else here is. It is the one behaviour whose
# failure mode is purely cosmetic: it cannot ping, cannot post in a channel, and
# says nothing an operator has to answer for.
PEREGRINE_ENABLE_PRESENCE=true

# How often the line quotes a WORD FROM THE CORPUS instead of reporting a count,
# as in "watching thoughts about ratio".
#
# That is user-typed text on public display with no conversation around it, which
# is the autonomous poster's category of risk rather than a lower one, so it goes
# through the emit gate like every other emission and falls back to a count when
# the gate refuses it. The word must be at least four characters, not a stop word,
# and said at least five times, so the bot cannot end up quoting noise.
#
# Set it to 0 if you do not want the bot's status to be user-derived at all.
PEREGRINE_PRESENCE_CORPUS_WORD_CHANCE=0.25

# PEREGRINE_ENABLE_CLUSTERING and PEREGRINE_CLUSTERING_TICK are RETIRED as of M7b
# and are deliberately not listed above. Concept clustering is deleted, not
# rebuilt, so there is no milestone left to defer them to and setting either now
# does nothing at all, not even warn.
#
# The reasoning is in SPEC.md section 8, finding 29, and the second point is the
# one worth knowing: the pass had never worked, but FIXING it would have made
# output worse rather than better. Its merge threshold of 0.005 collapses every
# name-seed into a single cluster, and that cluster fed the highest-weight tier of
# seed selection applied to every member, so a working codec would have made
# seeding roughly uniform over the name-adjacent vocabulary.
#
# What clusters actually offered, transitive association, is a bounded two-hop
# expansion inside seed selection now: computed per query, never stale, no bucket.

# How often to check whether the weekly leaderboard is due for a reset. The reset
# itself is weekly; this is only the polling interval.
PEREGRINE_LEADERBOARD_CHECK_TICK=1h

PEREGRINE_MAX_HISTORY=10000

# debug | info | warn | error
LOG_LEVEL=info

# ------------------------------------------------------------ transcription

# OFF by default, and the default deliberately differs from the old in-code
# constant. Transcription shells out to ffmpeg and whisper-cli and needs a
# 465 MiB model, none of which exist in a distroless image that has no shell at
# all. It stays a Windows-local development feature; the binaries and the model
# are gitignored and fetched by hand. See README.
#
# Enabling this in a container also requires a writable tmpfs at /tmp.
PEREGRINE_ENABLE_TRANSCRIPTION=false

# How many voice notes may be waiting at once. Transcription is slow and voice
# notes arrive in bursts, so this is what stops a burst becoming unbounded memory.
# A full queue drops the note and edits its placeholder to say so, rather than
# leaving it reading "in progress" forever.
PEREGRINE_TRANSCRIPTION_QUEUE=32
# These three configure a transcription ENGINE, and M12 shipped the seam rather
# than an engine: internal/plugins/voicenote defines an Engine interface and the
# only implementation in this repository reports itself unavailable. The one
# peregrine used to have needed a 465 MiB model and platform binaries that exist
# in no deployed environment, so the seam is what this repository can honestly own.
#
# They stay deferred rather than being deleted, because unlike the clustering
# variables there is a milestone that will read them. Setting the enable flag with
# no engine warns at startup and ignores voice notes; it does not fail silently.
# LATER (M12b)
PEREGRINE_WHISPER_MODEL=voicenotes/models/ggml-small.bin
# LATER (M12b)
PEREGRINE_VOICENOTES_DIR=voicenotes
# LATER (M12b)
PEREGRINE_TRANSCRIPTION_TIMEOUT=2m
